Hi Andrew,

Thanks for the detailed feedback. Addressing your points:

AS1: Our primary motivation is high-throughput backfilling (e.g.,
rehydrating state stores, training ML models). In these scenarios,
massive parallel reads are actually a feature, leveraging the
aggregate bandwidth of cloud storage (e.g., S3, GCS), which often
exceeds what a broker fleet can cost-effectively proxy.

Regarding RRR (KIP-1255) as a caching layer:
a) Cache hit rate for backfills would likely be low. Backfill
workloads typically involve a single consumer reading a unique offset
range rather than multiple consumers requesting the same historical
segments
b) RRR adds operation complexity (sizing, load distribution across RRR node=
s)
c) Direct fetch offers operational simplicity by avoiding this
separate tier entirely

That said, we're open to the community's view on whether RRR should be
prioritized based on what performs the best across the variety of
workloads.

AS2: Agreed, we updated the KIP to explicitly state share groups are
not supported. Share partition leader should maintain control of
distribution and state management.

AS3: We admit this does add client complexity. However, the
alternative is all remote fetches go through brokers, negating any
bandwidth or cost reduction benefits of KIP-1248. We view the plugin
as a necessary trade-off, similar to how Serializer/Deserializer
plugins exist client-side today. The plugins are also only opt-in for
users that need them, so most clients remain thin. The
SupportedStorageFormatVersions mechanism provides graceful degradation
if plugins don't match.

AS4: Thanks for calling this out. Our goal is to leverage the existing
transaction filtering logic in CompletedFetch without modification.
The key is ensuring the consumer receives the same data (byte range,
aborted transactions) that the broker would have returned if it had
mediated the fetch.

The broker uses the same RemoteLogManager.addAbortedTransactions flow
whether proxying data or returning segment location. It queries the
cached TransactionIndex and includes the list in FetchResponse.
Because the remote segments are immutable, the indexes are guaranteed
to match segment content. We'll add explicit coordination requirements
to the KIP and ensure rigorous testing across these boundaries.

Thanks for the thorough review.

Best,
Tom

