I'm glad to see that the Diskless Topic has entered the voting stage in
other threads.
KIP-1163: The design of Diskless Core retains LogSegment and Tiered
Storage.
My understanding is that the main problem solved by the current Diskless
Topic is
the cross-AZ traffic cost. If this is the main purpose, there might be a
simpler solution
that does not require modifications to Coordinator, Fetch, Transaction,
etc.
(I apologize for raising this question at this time. It's because there
were quite a lot of discussions about Diskless Topic before, and I didn't
keep up with them.) The new design is inspired by KIP-1163, KIP-1176 and
AutoMQ *Produce Path*:

   1. Producers send Produce requests to any broker (BrokerN).
   2. The BrokerN accumulates Produce requests in a buffer until exceeding
   some size or time limit.
   3. When enough data accumulates or the timeout elapses, the BrokerN
   upload a object for all of the buffered batches to object storage.
   4. BrokerN sends a ProxyRequest to BrokerM which is the real leader of
   the partition. The ProxyRequest contains the object key and the location
   information of the batches.
   5. BrokerM reads Batches from the object and writes them into Partition
   (Leader) as the classic topic. And caches the metadata <partition, offse=
t,
   timestamp, object, positionInObject> in memory for subsequent ISR
   replication.
   6. [ISR Replication] The Follower of the Partition sends a Fetch
   replication request to BrokerM.
   1. If BrokerM has a metadata cache of <partition, offset, timestamp,
      object, positionInObject>, return that metadata. The Follower reads t=
he
      object, combines it with the offset and timestamp to generate the fin=
al
      RecordBatch, and writes it into the LocalLog.
      2. If BrokerM does not have a metadata cache, then BrokerM returns
      the data of LocalLog (Leader) and sends it back.
      7. BrokerM moves the highwatermark.
   8. BrokerM responds ProxyResponse to BrokerN and BrokerN responds to
   Producer.

The object is only used to solve the cross-az traffic issued by Produce
forwarding and ISR replication.
There is no need for persistent storage of metadata, and it can be designed
to be cleaned up 1 minute after writing.
Even if it is accidentally deleted, it can still continue to work=EF=BC=9A

   - Accidentally deleted during the Produce forwarding phase: return a
   sending failure to the client, and the client will retry sending.
   - Accidentally deleted during the ISR replication phase: ISR replication
   can fall back to the replication mode of directly returning LocalLog dat=
a
   for the classic topic.

*Consume Path*:
It's the same as the classic topic *Transaction*:
It's the same as the classic topic *Tiered Storage*:
It's the same as the classic topic

There is no additional Coordinator, no need to rewrite a large amount of
Kafka logic for Diskless Topic, just use Object to eliminate cross-AZ
traffic.


