Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hi Robin,

Thanks for your design sketch.

Based on your description, the GET operations on the leader and followers
must complete before offsets are assigned, adding Produce request latency
that is not present in the KIP-1163 design.

KIP-1163 also assumes that object storage is more reliable than disk
storage, which is the case in the hyperscaler clouds. The parts of your
design that make it compatible with low-reliability object storage become
counterproductive when Diskless is used with a high-reliability object
storage, such as re-introducing direct replication and cross-az costs.

We=E2=80=99ve already discussed and rejected KIP-1176 (another =E2=80=9Csim=
pler=E2=80=9D proposal)
in favor of a complete solution. The folks who championed that proposal are
now working with us on KIP-1163, and are currently designing Compaction in
Tiered Storage to make Diskless a complete solution for compacted topics as
well. Similarly, we would appreciate your contributions to the design of
KIP-1163, and your vote on the motivational KIP-1150.

Thanks!

On Mon, Jan 19, 2026 at 10:18=E2=80=AFAM Ivan Yurchenko <ivan@ivanyu.me> wr=
ote:

> Hi Jun,
>
> Here are our answers to your questions and comments:
>
> > JR10. "Even in the case where network traffic is not accounted; we
> believe
> > that the operational benefits of Diskless topics are still appealing to
> > Kafka users."
> > Could you list other benefits such as better scalability and durability=
?
>
> Thank you, good point, added this.
>
> > JR11. "AWS charges 0.02$ per GiB"
> > The object store is not free and has a different cost model based on
> > requests. It would be useful summarize the tradeoff on cost and latency=
,
> > and when diskless topics are cost effective.
>
> Yes, there is a per-request cost for object storage which is not directly
> comparable to inter-zone transfer costs. We have found that the design in
> KIP-1163 amortizes those request costs such that they are insignificant
> compared to the $0.02/gb cost which is called out in KIP-1150.
> We currently have very precise cost models that were put together for an
> earlier iteration of the Diskless topics. These are somewhat stale and
> inaccurate for the latest design, so we will work in the background to po=
st
> concrete numbers in KIP-1163 before voting on that proposal. Would this b=
e
> OK?
>
> > JR12. Are the followup KIPs up to date? KIP-1164 and KIP-1181 haven't
> been
> > updated in the last 6 months. The metadata change in KIP-1181 is now
> > included in KIP-1163.
>
> Thanks for calling out. We have updated KIP-1164 with the latest design.
> It's missing public interfaces, but we think it may be a bit too early to
> define them as some rounds of discussion is ahead, with potential to chan=
ge
> the design. But otherwise the KIP is good for the first review.
> We have deprecated KIP-1181 as it's now incorporated into KIP-1163.
>
> Best,
> Ivan
