Hi Greg,

Thanks for the reply

> Based on your description, the GET operations on the leader and followers
> must complete before offsets are assigned, adding Produce request latency
> that is not present in the KIP-1163 design.

Yes, the ProduceRequestLatency is 1 * PUT(In Broker N) + 1 GET(In Broker M)
+ 1 GET(Follower Synchronization).
- Compared with KIP-1163: Produce Latency has two more GET time costs;
Consume E2E Latency has one more GET time cost.
- Compared with KIP-1163: There is no need for coordination by a
centralized Coordinator, thus eliminating the delay caused by Coordinator
confirmation and avoiding the impact of Coordinator single-point failure on
the availability of the entire cluster.

In the design of Diskless Topic, I suspect that adding the time cost of two
more GET operations will not bring about an essential change (with P50
increasing from 500ms to 700ms).

> KIP-1163 also assumes that object storage is more reliable than disk
> storage, which is the case in the hyperscaler clouds. The parts of your
> design that make it compatible with low-reliability object storage become
> counterproductive when Diskless is used with a high-reliability object
> storage, such as re-introducing direct replication and cross-az costs.

It might be that my description is not accurate enough. The design is also
strongly dependent on object storage. Regarding the part about object
management and recycling, what I want to emphasize more is that the current
design does not require complex object metadata management, and objects are
just a temporary state.

> We=E2=80=99ve already discussed and rejected KIP-1176 (another =E2=80=9Cs=
impler=E2=80=9D proposal)
> in favor of a complete solution.

I understand that the main reasons why "KIP-1176 was rejected" are that it
did not solve the cross-AZ traffic costs for Produce, and that implementing
Active Segment Tiered Storage added a lot of complexity to metadata
management.
The design I mentioned can not only solve all cross-AZ traffic costs
including sending and replication (the core goal of KIP-1150), but also
does not add complex management of Object metadata, nor does it require
large-scale modifications to Apache Kafka.
It could be a good design that can be adopted by KIP-1150.

